-- Recursion (SPEC §20, §21): `fib` calls itself, and nothing else is written. It is the fixed point
-- of its generator, whose calls of itself are direct calls of the self-reference it is given, and
-- a call of `fib` from outside is driven by `bounded`, the default driver: the kernel's
-- `bounded_fix`, a native recursion that carries a budget of depth, each call with one less.
-- `fib`'s row has `Exhausted`, what `bounded` performs when the budget is spent, where a loop's
-- has `div`; `main` handles it with `on_exhausted`.
--
-- Each level is one native call of known code, the budget a word passed down. Nothing is
-- allocated and nothing is dispatched (`corpus/counts.expected`: 0, 0, 0).
--
-- Answer: i32[610], fib(15). A budget that ran out gives 4294967295 (-1); a recursion that
-- answered a call with the wrong level gives another number.

include rec

fn fib(n: i32) -> i32 = if n < 2 { n } else { fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2) }

main -> i32 = with on_exhausted(!{ u -> 0 - 1 }) { fib(15) }
